### 🛑 Do not edit this file to change how agents work

**`AGENTS.md` is a bootstrap pointer, not the source of truth.** It exists to
get an agent oriented in its first minutes and to send it to the skill that
governs the task in front of it. When you want to change how agents behave,
edit the **skill**. A rule that exists only in an `AGENTS.md` is a rule with no
home: it is not in the generated reference, not in the published skill docs,
and not found by an agent that went looking for the skill first. That has
already happened — the turn-report discipline lived only in the root file until
2026-09-18. Sections that carry substantive rules rather than pointers are
**migration debt**, not precedent; adding to them widens it
([`placement`](skills/kg/kg-core/placement.md),
[the migration's survey](docs/proposals/agents-md-migration.md)).

#### Getting skills

Skills are knowledge-graph content, not a directory you memorise: they live in the `kg` graph an instance declares in its own
`<instance>.json` — here [`cat-harness.json`](cat-harness.json) — and a
downstream instance **inherits** its dependencies' skills through the same
declaration. Ask with `skill_list`, `skill_fetch` and `work_plan_prime`; with
no MCP, resolve the `kg` graph from the declaration
([`kg-navigation`](skills/kg/kg-navigation/kg-navigation.md),
[`directory-conventions`](skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md)).
**A dependency's skills ARE reachable** — `resolveSkillDirs` in
`schemas/harness-config.ts` computes the cross-instance overlay and
`LOCAL_PACKAGES` in `scripts/skill-packages.ts` is built from it. Two nearby
call sites stay root-only on purpose, and the reasons are on the overlay
function itself: `kgDirectories` composes repo-relative skill ids, and
`kgRoots(root)[0]` is read as the root's graph while overlay order is
deepest-dependency-first.

Start an onboarding at [`docs/guides/agent-onboarding.md`](docs/guides/agent-onboarding.md),
published at
<https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/docs/cat-harness/guides/agent-onboarding.html>.

### Content types, and starting a folio

A **document** folio is structured prose; a **paper** is that plus the block
kinds whose assertion is a formal claim, backed by `.lean` siblings. Adapters
partition and profiles nest — [`content-profiles`](skills/authoring/authoring-core/content-profiles.md).
A new folio is `bun run cat init-folio --help` or the `folio_init` tool, which
is registered among the **generic** tools because it runs before a folio has a
content type; why block manifests import a builder shim rather than the
platform is in [`memory/`](memory/the-builder-shim-and-why-folio-init-is-generic.md),
and the walk-through is [`writing-a-document`](docs/guides/writing-a-document.md).

### Commands, and the gate set

The commands are listed in [`README.md`](README.md#running-it). Two are worth an
agent's attention. **`bun run cat gates` before you push** — it derives its list
from `.github/workflows/code-quality-gates.yml`, and **a subset of the gate set
is not the gate set** ([`platform-gates`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/platform-gates.md)).
**`bun run cat preview:site`**, because a green gate set is not a rendered page
([`rendered-verification`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/rendered-verification.md), bean `gjli`).

### Where the harness keeps its state — `beans/` is a graph

`beans/` is a graph with named nodes, declared by `beans/beans.json` (schema
`schemas/bean-graph.ts`), and a file declares what it is: a bean by its front
matter, a workflow instance by `"$schema": "folio-workflow-instance/v1"`.
**Extension is a coincidence; a declaration inside the file is the contract.**
The two duplicates of the layout that cannot be removed are both checked by
`bun run cat check:harness-dirs`: `.beans.yml`, because the `beans` binary is
third-party, and `WORKFLOW_DIR` in `src/workflow/store.ts`, because it is on the
hot path of every workflow call. These were `.beans/` and `.harness/workflow/`
until 2026-09-18 (beans `8xzw`, `x89g`), and `.harness/` is gone — a
dot-prefixed path is what this layer's own guard rejects. The tracked graphs
are kept on state branches now and mounted by the session-start hook
(`bun run cat state:mount`). QA verdicts are on the orphan `qa-reports` branch;
judgements stay in `test/attestations/`
([`directory-conventions`](skills/kg/kg-core/directory-conventions.md),
[`workflow-state`](skills/process/workflow/workflow-state.md),
[`qa-reports`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/qa-reports.md),
[the proposal](docs/proposals/workflow-state-in-beans.md)).

### Work-plan & todos — use `beans`

One todo mechanism for agent work, session-local and cross-session alike
([`todo-manager`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/todo-manager.md),
[`bean-coordination`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/bean-coordination.md)). Two
spellings to avoid: `beans <id> --status in-progress` **exits 1 with `unknown
command`**, and `beans update <id> --status in-progress` is not a claim (it
writes no holder note — use `bun run cat beans:claim <id>`). `beans update` stays
right for closing your own bean, `--body-append` and `--blocked-by`. Never
delete a bean — `scrapped`, with reasons — which is one instance of
[`deletion-requires-confirmation`](skills/conduct/conduct-core/deletion-requires-confirmation.md).
Bean updates are pushed directly to the branch store
(`bun cat-harness-tools/scripts/branch-store.ts push --id beans` or `bun run cat state:push`)
rather than committed to code PRs on `main`.
Beans are not sidecars, and this is not the content-review feedback workflow
([`todo-review`](skills/authoring/authoring-core/todo-review.md)).

### At session start

Get `beans` in hand before any durable work (step 1 above). If it will not
install you are still not read-only: `scripts/beans-fallback.ts` writes the same
store in the same layout. The session-start sweep
(`scripts/session-start-coord-sweep.sh`) prints a copy-paste line for resuming;
set `BEANS_CHECKOUT_ROOT` if your clones live under one predictable directory
and it will name the path there. What the sweep emits, and in what order, is
[`todo-manager`](skills/sdlc/sdlc-core/todo-manager.md).

